    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d59-a4">

                <pb facs="0117" n="771" xml:id="pg771"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure 4 in No. 132.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Seward to Mr.
                            Bingham.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >United States Consulate-General</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Shanghai</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1874-09-01"><hi rendition="#italic">September</hi> 1,
                        1874</date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 24.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to hand yon herewith a
                    draught of a dispatch which has been addressed by the several consuls at this
                    port to their several ministers at Peking, looking to the need of action to
                    secure the safety of our commerce and of our settlements in the event of war
                    between China and Japan.</p>
                <p>I am, sir, your obedient servant,</p>
                <closer><signed>GEO. F. SEWARD,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Consul-General.</hi></signed></closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p>Hon. <hi rendition="#small-caps">John A. Bingham</hi>,<lb/><hi
                            rendition="#italic">United States Minister to Japan, Yeddo.</hi></p>
                </postscript>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
